#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09021ce8-047e-5b0d-92f8-36d32076c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519005} {:answer/id #uuid "2d58aa30-649a-5d30-86fd-836c82705dcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519065} {:answer/id #uuid "7bce9ce0-a3ff-570e-86db-d819bc4f24bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519187} {:answer/id #uuid "99059b85-08c0-5911-bef5-b258cf55a646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519244} {:answer/id #uuid "99eaaeec-7b23-5191-b1ac-c727b2e8bc60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09021ce8-047e-5b0d-92f8-36d32076c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519005} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8d7f1f-3c0d-5b85-87f8-4a2364ded62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d34b6be0-737c-5c5c-907f-c713ce8e6b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-133236" :question/problem "The average height of a player in a particular professional basketball league is $78$ inches. Additionally, $15%$ of players are over $82$ inches tall. Assuming that a player's height is normally distributed, find the percentage of players over $75$ inches tall, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ | $\\,0.2148\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected player. Then we have $X∼N(78,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>75).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>82)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =1-P(X>82) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{82-78}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2591 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2591} \\\\  & ≈3.85951.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>75)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>75) & =1-P(X\\le 75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{75-78}{3.85951}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le -0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.78) \\\\  & =1-0.2177 \\\\  & =0.7823 \\\\  & ≈78.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $78.2%$ of players are over $75$ inch tall."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f88efb-26a7-5927-a3a0-854d67c40077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f88efb-26a7-5927-a3a0-854d67c40077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9151a6ca-f2c2-5987-ba7e-6def5ae3cbbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6b501ec-18d5-51e8-9a88-2c1c73e44bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-324738" :question/problem "The lifespan of LED light bulbs is normally distributed with a mean of $40,000$ hours and an unknown standard deviation $\\sigma .$ Tests show that $10%$ of bulbs fail before $36,000$ hours. Assuming a normal model, find the standard deviation, **rounded to the nearest integer.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,z\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.8$ | $0.85$ | $0.9$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$h$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifespan of an LED bulb. Then we have $X∼N(40,000,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<36,000)=0.10.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<36,000) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{36,000-40,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ -\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)=1.2816,\n$$\n\nand therefore, by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)=-1.2816.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =-1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4,000}{1.2816} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3,121\\,\\text{h.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1103e6c4-8e5a-5d60-a8d6-1e582b31d670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357894} {:answer/id #uuid "4beccc4b-bd42-53f7-a6ef-d29833929fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358408} {:answer/id #uuid "58efc89e-0bb9-5250-bf2e-d9981f1e00d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358525} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0097db-0220-5610-aa9c-bf8496bfb7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359833} {:answer/id #uuid "ee51d506-3a45-567d-86d2-6491d5d72676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58efc89e-0bb9-5250-bf2e-d9981f1e00d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358525} :answer-field/id #uuid "79790386-5ca3-5b7e-a326-b45ba6bd2fed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5563fda7-bd6a-55bd-869b-0a5d94077bb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95024" :question/problem "The time that a particular laptop battery lasts is normally distributed with a mean of $32$ hours and a standard deviation of $4$ hours. What is the probability that a full charge lasts for $36$ hours or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the time the full charge lasts. Then we have $X∼N(32,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 36).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 36) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{36-32}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1)=1-0.8413≈0.1587.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a full charge lasts $36$ hours or more is approximately $0.16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf6bb84-99f4-501b-898f-55beecfd0ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519011} {:answer/id #uuid "6e877167-064e-5961-ba0d-9292251730e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519157} {:answer/id #uuid "a07531b8-8752-5b56-b8df-4b8c0357540d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519253} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0bfe5f-9010-5b76-900e-a0a88d617f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519356} {:answer/id #uuid "f64b54b4-f561-592c-ab77-9cb0208b121a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc0bfe5f-9010-5b76-900e-a0a88d617f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519356} :answer-field/id #uuid "c153adbb-ebdd-563f-aa07-ecead9219cce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e64f0682-cd52-5135-b0b3-08e76e7a1873" :question/math-academy-id "q-95082" :question/problem "A group of students took a test and got an average score of $79.$ Also, $15%$ of the students scored lower than $66.$ Assuming that the scores of the students are normally distributed, find the standard deviation of their scores, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(79,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 66)=0.15.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 66) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{66-79}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{13}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364,\n$$\n\nand therefore by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(1-0.85)={Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{-13}{-1.0364} \\\\  & ≈12.54.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032b45ef-0f8d-5228-ab53-44d951ab7b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518991} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef38244-8fdf-5af9-a467-8e572c929ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519015} {:answer/id #uuid "3f275ff8-361f-502e-8e26-7ac774e7b63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519093} {:answer/id #uuid "c3efee66-91d8-51df-9c99-66043ccf9a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519316} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a1db81-4260-5c90-a443-a427b1dbd8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b45ef-0f8d-5228-ab53-44d951ab7b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518991} :answer-field/id #uuid "d314da54-c3a8-55ac-8066-a2906576f626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "217980d1-12ff-5532-90b2-461f43899f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-95091" :question/problem "A truck travels an average distance of $21\\,\\text{mi}$ with a gallon of gas. However, $15%$ of the time, a truck travels more than $26\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Assuming that the distance the truck can travel with a gallon of gas is normally distributed, find, as a percent, the proportion of journeys that the truck travels less than $15\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0918\\,$ | $\\,0.0901\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the distance traveled by the truck with a gallon of gas. Then we have $X∼N(21,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X\\le 15).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>26)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =1-P(X>26) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{26-21}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2073 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2073} \\\\  & ≈4.8239.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\le 15)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 15) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{15-21}{4.8239}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.24) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.24) \\\\  & =0.1075 \\\\  & ≈10.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $10.8%$ of the time, a gallon of gas travels only $15\\,\\text{mi}$ or less."}]}
